== Finding 3 — attestation provenance could be more specific ==

[[Attestation]] correctly separates cryptographic/verifiable provenance from truth of the attested claim.

Its third reference used a generic “NIST remote-attestation and platform-attestation literature” label.

The audit replaces that generic source-family reference with:

* IETF RFC 9334, ''Remote ATtestation procedureS (RATS) Architecture'' (2023).

RFC 9334 supplies a concrete attester → evidence → verifier → attestation result → relying-party architecture and reinforces the article's distinction between evidence/appraisal and truth by assertion.

== Finding 4 — recent standards publication state is already correctly labeled ==

No correction is required to the recent identity/credential standards references:

* W3C DID Core v1.0 — Recommendation, 19 July 2022.
* W3C DID Core v1.1 — Candidate Recommendation Snapshot, 5 March 2026.
* W3C Verifiable Credentials Data Model v2.0 — Recommendation, 15 May 2025.
* W3C Verifiable Credentials Data Model v2.1 — Working Draft, 5 September 2026.

The v2.1 Working Draft remains explicitly a work in progress and is cited only for current terminology evolution, not as a finished Recommendation.

The EMBL-EBI introductory bioinformatics pathway is also correctly identified as reviewed April 2026.

== Cross-wave semantic audit ==

=== Wave 10 → Wave 11 ===

[[Governance]] remains a broad institutional concept; blockchain governance is only one application.

[[Incentive]], [[Game theory]], and [[Mechanism design]] can help analyze consensus and DeFi mechanisms without defining [[Consensus mechanism]], [[Automated market maker]], or [[Decentralized finance]].

[[Sybil attack]] remains a general distributed-systems/security concept. Its relationship to [[Consensus mechanism]], [[Reputation system]], and decentralized identity systems does not make it blockchain-specific.

[[Identity]] remains broader than [[Decentralized identity]], wallet addresses, public keys, and token ownership.

=== Wave 11 market boundaries ===

The combined corpus preserves:

<pre>
AMM ≠ liquidity pool
AMM ≠ constant-product mechanism only
liquidity pool ≠ liquidity provider
slippage ≠ price impact
price impact ≠ fee
arbitrage ≠ guaranteed executable profit
oracle ≠ truth
attestation ≠ truth
token ≠ external asset itself
wallet ≠ on-chain asset storage
</pre>

These distinctions survive cross-linking and do not require new identities.

=== Wave 11 → Wave 12 ===

No forced conceptual bridge is added merely because the waves are consecutive.

Biological information and computational biology reuse general concepts such as [[Information]], [[Database]], [[Metadata]], [[Provenance]], [[Algorithm]], [[Software]], [[Version control]], [[Network]], and [[Scientific model]].

They do not require blockchain-specific concepts to define their public scope.

Sparse cross-wave connectivity here is therefore semantically correct rather than a graph defect.

=== Wave 12 internal boundaries ===

The combined corpus preserves:

<pre>
genetics ≠ genomics
genomics ≠ sequencing
biological sequence ≠ physical molecule
sequence ≠ annotation
alignment ≠ homology
similarity ≠ homology
database record ≠ verified truth
predicted annotation ≠ experimentally validated function
phylogenetic tree ≠ directly observed history
gene tree ≠ species tree
systems biology ≠ network diagram
reproducible computational research ≠ reproducibility
reproducibility ≠ replication
</pre>

